i am nick gillespie with reason TV and  today we're talking with Veronique de  Russia she's a reason columnist and an  economist for the Mercatus Center at  George Mason University frequent  collaborator with me Veronique thanks  for coming thanks for having me we want  to talk about austerity there is a  growing backlash against austerity  measures in governments especially in  Europe you have people like Larry  Summers you have people like Paul  Krugman saying look Europe has tried  estera t measures and their economies  are tanking they're going down like the  Titanic what is being talked about when  people say that austerity has been tried  in Europe what these guys are saying is  that the reason why Europe is doing very  poorly is because they've tried to cut  spending dramatically right but really  when you actually look at what happened  in Europe they've tried what's here we  call the balanced approach which is like  a mix of spending cuts and tax increase  when I think of austerity what I figure  as the government is just saying you  know what we spent a billion dollars  this year we're spending 500 million  next year that's not what's happening in  Europe well that's not even what how  most people will describe austerity what  they their goal is and that's how like  most budgets are even written is there  set targets for reducing the debt to GDP  ratio and you can do it in many ways you  can you know cut spending but you can  also do a mix of spending cuts tax  increases or just tax increases Krugman  in particular has looked at England and  said you know what they tried austerity  the camera government was talking about  cutting spending and luck their economy  they're gone back in recession what's  wrong with that analysis well first I  mean when Krugman talks about austerity  in the UK or in Europe in general he  implies that it's only spending cuts  that were put in place he never ever  talk about the tax increases which were  quite substantial also he talks about  what Cameron has actually said he was  going to do not what has really been  done and what Cameron it's true its  proposed to do was he was going to  dramatically cut spending it was going  to be something like  nineteen percent across the board for  each departments he was going to engage  in serious pension reforms he was going  to reduce taxes the reality when you  look at the data is actually spending  his gun up I mean it hasn't grown as  fast as it used to but it still has gone  up England and the rest of Europe is a  good example of what the balanced  approach is when you look at these guys  austerity measures when you look at  charts that they produce it is really  almost a quelle amount of spending cuts  and tax increases they're going to close  deficits and reduce that to gdp by they  say they're going to cut spending a  little bit and they're going to raise  taxes they're going to cut spending and  they often have very ambitious spending  cuts in mind and they're going to raise  taxes but what ends up happening is this  the tax increases are real but then in  most cases the spending cuts don't  materialize in cameron's cases for  instance he had talked about reforming  pensions to be concrete well two years  later they still has been no no reform  of pensions because people have been you  know interest great in interest groups  were not on board and so he's not pushed  it through so you also in your work  you've referenced economic research that  looks at past successful cases where  countries were able to reduce the debt  to GDP ratio and they issue a balanced  approach what what do they use and  what's the effect of that on you know  whether or not a country goes into  recession or not so there's a series of  economics who have actually looked at  this at this question is like what type  of austerity works or doesn't work and  for instance alberto alesina at harvard  and Sylvia are Daria they've looked at  107 attempts to reduce debt to GDP ratio  in in all the countries in the OECD over  the last 30 years right and what they  found is at the successful debt  reduction package of fiscal adjustment  package were the one that were made  exclusively of spending cuts what  they've shown is that the balanced  approach to basically the country that  have tried to reduce that to GDP ratio  by doing a mix of spending cuts and tax  increases have fell may be more relevant  also is the fact that they've shown that  packages that were made of spending cuts  they were actually more likely to lead  to an expansion to economic growth while  the one that were made well when you try  to actually stimulate the economy  through spending you were less likely to  get an expansion people always when they  look to Europe they never see you know  they always talk about Greece they was  talking about Italy they typically talk  about countries that have had flagging  economies for a very long time they  never talked about Germany which you  know is the leading economy in Europe  really in terms of energy and activity  what is Germany doing that the rest of  the world or at least the rest of Europe  should be thinking about emulating  Germany is actually an example for  America because Germany is by many  accounts now doing better than us they  have a six point two percent  unemployment rate what they've done is  that in two in 2004 they've actually  engaged in serious structural reform  because it's another thing that  economists have found when they looked  at what kind of you know fiscal  adjustment packages work and they found  that structural reforms were and by that  you mean it's not just saying okay we're  cutting 10% next year it's that we're  changing spending patterns that  governments are locked into but not that  only it's also labor labor market  reforms think about it I mean labor  market laws I mean direct how easily is  going to be to get a job start a  business fire someone who doesn't work  but also the type of benefits that  people who are unemployed are going to  get right and so what German what  Germany is done in 2004 I think it was  if they dramatically reform their labor  market so that's what they call the mini  jobs you know those jobs that are not  necessarily very lucrative are easily to  get by to get that you can go in and out  of the workforce more and also that  there's much fewer incentives to stay  unemployed so they've reduced the the  the unemployment benefits and they've  done the second thing that is very  important is that they've reduced the  size and pay of government employees  well thank you very much for talking to  reason TV about austerity and its  discontents for reason TV I'm nickel SP
 

